<td id="S4.T2.7.1.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">✓</td>
<td id="S4.T2.7.1.15.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t">-</td>
<td id="S4.T2.7.1.15.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.1.15.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">85.3</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.7.1.15.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.1.15.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">85.1</span></td>
<td id="S4.T2.7.1.15.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.1.15.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">85.2</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Comparative Study of Training Algorithms</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our proposed Tree-GRPO, we compared it against MCTS combined with DPO and SFT on ALFWorld. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, Tree-GRPO (blue line) consistently achieves superior task success rates (Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>a) and greater efficiency with fewer average steps (Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>b) than MCTS + DPO (purple line) and MCTS + SFT (red line). This demonstrates the enhanced performance of integrating GRPO within our MCTS-driven self-evolving framework for long-horizon embodied tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted an ablation study to demonstrate that our SEEA-R1 can achieve sustained performance improvement by leveraging its internally trained Multi-Modal Generative Reward Model (MGRM), thereby reducing reliance on ground truth (GT)rewards from the simulator. We compared three critical configurations with results shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21669v1/figs/0.png" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison of SEEA-R1 using different optimization algorithms on ALFWorld over training iterations, more detailed figures are provided in Appendix.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEEA-R1 with GT Rewards:</span> Policy optimization is guided directly by GT environment rewards. As expected, this (typically the blue line) yields the highest success rate, establishing an upper bound due to the perfectly accurate reward signal.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEEA-R1 with frozen MGRM:</span> An untrained and frozen MGRM provides reward signals for policy optimization. Notably, the orange line shows a high initial success rate in the first iteration despite the inaccurate reward signal. It’s possible that the initial exploration coincidentally aligned with the flawed reward. Nevertheless, the fundamental unreliability of the frozen MGRM ultimately misguides the learning process, leading to performance degradation in later iterations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SEEA-R1 with training MGRM:</span> Our proposed method, where the MGRM is trained (accuracy reported in Appendix) through the self-evolution process and then used to guide policy optimization. This configuration (e.g., green line) demonstrates consistent and significant performance improvement, approaching the results achieved with direct GT rewards.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The key finding from Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study: Self-Evolution with MGRM ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is that SEEA-R1, when utilizing its self-trained MGRM, can effectively learn and enhance its policy. This highlights the MGRM’s capability to provide reliable internal reward signals, enabling robust self-evolution and continuous performance gains without persistent dependence on external GT environment rewards.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.21669v1/figs/reward_models_success_rate_.png" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:947/587;" width="947" height="587" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of success rates of SEEA-R1 under different reward configurations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.4 </span>Generalization on Out-of-Distribution Embodied Benchmark</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To assess the generalization capabilities of proposed SEEA-R1, we conducted evaluations on the EmbodiedEval benchmark, an out-of-distribution (OOD) multi-modal embodied simulation test set. The success rates are reported in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 4.2.4 Generalization on Out-of-Distribution Embodied Benchmark ‣ 4.2 Experimental Results ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ SEEA-R1: Tree-Structured Reinforcement Fine-Tuning for Self-Evolving Embodied Agents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
Notably, our SEEA-R1 method, when fine-tuned with Tree-GRPO, demonstrates significantly improved performance after training. It achieves an overall success rate of 19.88%, which not only shows an uplift compared to the base model (Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct at 18.29%) but also markedly outperforms models fine-tuned with SFT (16.77%) and DPO (16.77%). These results underscore the strong generalization performance of Tree-GRPO approach on this challenging, unseen benchmark.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.T3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Success Rate in Embodiedeval benchmark</span></figcaption>
